rage.\n   - Space: O(n) in the worst case (all elements are unique).\n\n6.  **Refine & Check Constraints:**\n   - Concise? Yes.\n   - Returns index of first duplicate? Yes.\n   - Returns -1 if none? Yes.\n   - Includes complexity? Yes.\n   - Edge cases: Empty iterable, all unique, all same, non-hashable elements? The problem says \"iterable\", but set requires hashable elements. I should mention this limitation or handle it, but for a concise answer, assuming hashable elements is standard. I'll add a brief note if needed, but keep it tight.\n\n   Let's verify the \"first duplicate\" definition. If input is `[1, 2, 3, 2, 1]`, the first duplicate encountered is `2` at index 3. My code returns 3. That's correct.\n   What if they want the index of the *first occurrence* of the duplicated element? Usually, \"index of the first duplicate\" means the index where the duplication is first detected. I'll stick with that. I can clarify in one line if needed, but it's fine.\n\n   Code looks solid. Complexity is standard. I'll format it concisely.\n\n7.  **"
